# Beat: guided walk through bob/logfile against alice/app.desc.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set alice/app.desc bob/logfile \
#       --script beats/app.desc
#
# The root type is not in alice/app.desc — it is Bob's own log envelope,
# which reproto recovered no schema for.  The session opens untyped.
# This beat names the structure by hand, enables heat cues, and follows
# the cues as far as alice/app.desc can take them.
#
# Re-pin (must print nothing):
#   protolens --descriptor-set alice/app.desc bob/logfile \
#       --script beats/app.desc script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: The logfile, app.desc

steps:
  - text: |
      bob/logfile, opened against alice/app.desc.

      This protobuf is larger, and protolens did not find an obvious type for it.
      Instead of a type name, a generic `message` indication is displayed.
      Let's fold it, so that all its top-level messages fit on screen.

      👋 Press `1` to fold the protobuf.

    node: /
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - not:
        - visible: /1/1

  - text: |
      We see four top-level messages with field number 1.
      The last one is flagged TRUNCATED —
      that is why protoc was not able to decode the log file.

      Let's zoom a little bit.

      👋 Press `2` to see one level deeper.

    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - visible: /1/1

  - text: |
      The top-level messages do look like log entries.
      For clarity, let's override the generic `message` indication
      with a synthetic type, and call it Entry.

      👋 Press Enter to commit the prefilled override.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "1 [{]  #@ message"
    command: override /:1 --as-new Entry --cardinality repeated --field-name entries
    advance_when:
      - type: /1 Entry

  - text: |
      👍 The override is in place.

      To go further, let's enable the heat cue system.

      protolens will highlight every message with a cue showing how well the corpus
      can type it. The brighter the cue, the more confident the match.

      👋 Press `i` to enable the heat cues (Inferred types).

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "entries [{]  #@ repeated Entry"
    advance_when:
      - heat_cues: findings

  - text: |
      Here we see a bright heat cue for field number 5. Let's hover over it.
      protolens proposes two candidates —
      and field number 2, just above, names the one we want: SearchTextResponse.

      Let's choose it.

      👋 Double-click the heat cue, then double-click SearchTextResponse.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/4
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/4 google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextResponse

  - text: |
      👍 The content seems to match (press `Z` to unfold the message)

      👋 Now click field number 4 just above.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/4
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - node: /1/3

  - text: |
      Hovering over the heat cue gives us SearchTextRequest.

      This makes sense: this is an RPC request/response pair.

      👋 Double-click the heat cue, and choose SearchTextRequest.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/3
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/3 google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest

  - text: |
      👍 SearchTextRequest is locked in.

      Field 2: a string. Looks like the RPC method name.
      Let's just name it "rpc".

      👋 Press Enter to commit the prefilled override.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/2
    select_line: true
    command: override Entry:2 --field-name rpc
    advance_when:
      - field_name: /1/2 rpc

  - text: |
      Field number 1? Hover over the heat cue...
      Multiple choices... because it is such a small message.
      Timestamp — fits a log entry.

      👋 Press `t`, choose Timestamp, press Enter to commit.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /1/1
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/1 google.protobuf.Timestamp

  - text: |
      Now look at the 3rd entry — fields 6 and 7.
      Negative scores 😨 and unconvincing heat cues 🤔.

      Our corpus only knows the Places API, but hmm: `ComputeRoutes` —
      that looks like another one of the Google APIs.
      We should reopen the protobuf against the full Google APIs corpus.

      👋 Press space to step.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /3/3
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      Before we do that, let's save our overrides.
      We do not want to redo this work.

      👋 Press `s` and save under alice/overrides.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /3/3
    advance_when:
      - file_exists: alice/overrides

  - text: |
      All done.

      👋 Press Enter to quit.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    command: quit
